  "notes": ["the client side and the server side are two instances of the own TCP state machine in one process, joined by an in memory pair of frame queues of fps-backend-afxdp, so there is no kernel socket and no descriptor", "frames are Ethernet (802.1Q tagged when --own-vlan is set) with IPv4 and TCP, with an IP header checksum and a TCP checksum that are computed on send and verified on receive; the MAC address of host i of a pool is mac_base + i and the neighbor of an address is read from the HST-03 table of fps-hosts", "the client side is a pool of hosts (--own-pool) and the server side a pool of hosts (--own-server-hosts); the connection table is keyed by the 4-tuple of both addresses and both ports, and a connect takes the next client host in turn", "retransmission is minimal: one timer per connection, a fixed initial timeout that doubles, go back N, no round trip estimate and no fast retransmit, and the link loses nothing, so the measurements never retransmit", "there is no congestion control, no window scaling (the window is at most 65,535 bytes), no delayed acknowledgment, and no out of order queue", "timers live in a heap with one entry per connection, so a poll with idle sockets does not grow with the socket count, where a stack that walks its sockets does", "a closed tuple is held for one second in TIME-WAIT instead of minutes, so a rate run does not run out of ports", "bytes_per_socket is the growth of the resident set of the process and includes the connection table, the buffers that the held sockets own (none while idle), and the frame link", "sockets counts both hosts of a pair and includes the listening sockets"],
